# maspsx fix: --jtbl-at-fusion, an opt-in peephole for the systemic
# jump-table `$at` codegen mismatch (default OFF, w23-a11 toolchain
# investigation, 2026-07-24).
#
# SYMPTOM: for every `switch` compiled to a jump table (`lw $reg,
# jtbl_XXXX(idx)`), the ORACLE always shows aspsx 2.77's indexed-symbol-load
# MACRO expansion via $at:
#
#     sll  $v0, $idx, 2
#     lui  $at, %hi(jtbl_XXXX)
#     addu $at, $at, $v0
#     lw   $v0, %lo(jtbl_XXXX)($at)
#
# but our pipeline (cc1psx/cc1plpsx -> maspsx -> GNU as) always emits an
# explicit 2-register form instead (never touching $at):
#
#     lui   $v0, %hi(jtbl_XXXX)   # high
#     addiu $v0, $v0, %lo(jtbl_XXXX) # low
#     sll   $v1, $idx, 2
#     addu  $v1, $v1, $v0
#     lw    $v0, 0($v1)
#
# 33-49 oracle sites tree-wide (`grep -rl jtbl_ asm/nonmatchings`); each
# costs ~5 extra insns + a register-pressure ripple.
#
# ROOT CAUSE (layer diagnosis -- the actual deliverable of this
# investigation): the divergence is NEITHER a maspsx bug NOR a GNU-as
# quirk. It is CC1PSX ITSELF. gcc's MIPS "casesi" RTL pattern (how it
# lowers a switch-statement jump-table dispatch) is a HARD-CODED asm
# template that always manually expands the table-address computation into
# 5 explicit machine instructions using a normal GPR as scratch -- it NEVER
# emits the assembler's symbol+register indexed-load pseudo-op (`lw $r,
# sym($idx)`) that would let the assembler pick $at. This was confirmed by
# capturing cc1's raw pre-maspsx .s output for PadInfoAct
# (recon/syslib/psx/libpad/PADENTRY.c) directly:
#
#     lui   $2,%hi($L38) # high
#     addiu $2,$2,%lo($L38) # low
#     sll   $3,$3,2
#     addu  $3,$3,$2
#     lw    $2,0($3)
#
# (cc1 lowers a *plain* array/struct index the exact same way -- this shape
# is generic "materialize a scaled-index base address" codegen, not
# switch-specific; the fusion below is scoped to jump tables only, see
# below.)
#
# GO/NO-GO FACT (checked BEFORE writing this peephole, per the
# investigation brief): does GNU as's own $at macro expansion of the
# *equivalent* single-line pseudo-op byte-match ASPSX 2.77's oracle
# sequence?  Hand-assembled test (`lw $2, jtbl_test($2)` after a `sll`)
# through our actual `mipsel-none-elf-as.exe` + objdump:
#
#     sll  v0,a0,0x2
#     lui  at,%hi(jtbl_test)
#     addu at,at,v0
#     lw   v0,%lo(jtbl_test)(at)
#
# -- byte-identical shape to the oracle. GO. Better still, maspsx ALREADY
# performs exactly this expansion for any symbol+register indexed load
# (see the `is_addend and r_source` branch of `process_line`, the
# non-`addiu_at` sub-case): `lui $at,%hi(sym); addu $at,$at,r_source;
# {op} $r,%lo(sym)($at)`. So no new expansion code is needed at all -- this
# peephole only has to re-fuse cc1's manual 5-instruction lowering back
# into the one-line pseudo-op maspsx's EXISTING machinery already expands
# correctly (including the load-delay nop insertion before a dependent
# `j`/`jr`, via the existing `_handle_nop_before_next_instruction`/
# `uses_at()` logic).
#
# WHY A MASPSX PEEPHOLE (not a cc1/build-flag fix): the casesi asm
# template is baked into gcc's machine description, not reachable via any
# CC1PSX flag this project's driver exposes; a maspsx-side re-fusion is the
# right layer, consistent with maspsx's whole purpose (reproduce ASPSX-era
# assembler/macro behaviors GNU as's naive passthrough doesn't).
#
# MATCHING QUIRK -- cc1 sometimes schedules the sequence's first
# instruction (`lui`) INTO THE DELAY SLOT of a preceding branch (e.g. the
# switch's bounds-check `sltiu ...; beqz ...`), splitting it from `addiu`
# by a `.set noreorder/nomacro ... .set macro/reorder` bracket + blank
# line. The oracle does the same thing, just with `sll` (not `lui`) as the
# filler -- confirming ASPSX performed the identical re-fusion. The peephole
# below matches on REAL-INSTRUCTION adjacency (skipping `.set`/blank/
# comment/label scaffolding) so it fires correctly in both the flat and
# delay-slot-split cases, and does an in-place replace+delete so the
# branch's delay-slot bracket is left structurally intact (now filled by
# `sll` instead of `lui`, matching the oracle).
#
# SCOPE / CONSERVATISM: the 5-instruction pattern match additionally
# requires the loaded register to feed a computed jump (`j`/`jr $R`)
# shortly after the load. A plain array/struct load is never followed by a
# jump through the loaded value, so this cannot misfire on ordinary
# indexed loads (which use the identical instruction SHAPE but are not
# jump tables, and were NOT part of this investigation's scope or
# verification). Default OFF at the maspsx-flag level (`--jtbl-at-fusion`).
#
# w24-a9 UPDATE (2026-07-25): the retail binary's jtbl sites turned out to be
# MIXED -- of the tree-wide 33 `jtbl_`-bearing oracle fns
# (`grep -l jtbl_ asm/nonmatchings/main/*.s`), only 11 TUs carry the ASPSX
# $at macro form this peephole targets; the other 22 already emit our
# explicit 5-insn form and must NOT be fused (enabling globally regresses
# them). So the wiring changed: NFS4_JTBL_AT_FUSION=1 (global, all TUs) is
# now only a blunt override for one-off testing/bisection; the real gate is
# `tools/build.py`'s `PER_TU_FLAGS` dict (`{"jtbl_at_fusion": True}` per
# source path), which scopes `--jtbl-at-fusion` to exactly the 11 TUs that
# need it. See build.py's PER_TU_FLAGS comment for the current TU list.
#
# VERIFIED (2026-07-24/25, isolated copy C:/Temp/maspsx-a9/ only -- NOT
# applied to the shared C:/Temp/maspsx-master/ used by other agents'
# concurrent builds), all 11 $at-form fns, before -> after (mangled name
# used where the recon free fn isn't `extern "C"`; two libcd/libmcrd
# internal helpers have an ours-name/oracle-name mismatch pre-existing in
# recon, unrelated to this fix -- see w24-a9 commit d1059dc for detail):
#   AudioCmn_SoundCar             FAIL 682 (532/530) -> FAIL 689 (531/530)
#   CD_get_intr (_cd_get_intr)    FAIL 463 (340/343) -> FAIL 460 (339/343)
#   FntPrint                      FAIL 103 (241/240) -> FAIL  98 (240/240)
#   HighExecute__10AIHigh_Cop     FAIL 240 (1450/1460)-> FAIL 247 (1449/1460)
#   HighExecute__14AIHigh_Traffic FAIL 794 (433/547) -> FAIL 801 (432/547)
#   MemCardCmd_cb (_..._cb__FPv)  FAIL 140 (111/141) -> FAIL 135 (110/141)
#   PadInfoAct                    FAIL  25 ( 54/ 53) -> FAIL  20 ( 53/ 53)
#   R3DCar_InsertCarFacet         FAIL 1388(1168/1144)-> FAIL 1395(1167/1144)
#   Replay_GetInterfaceKey        FAIL 280 (296/292) -> FAIL 287 (295/292)
#   Sfx_BuildSouffleFacet         FAIL 1339(877/938) -> FAIL 1346 (876/938)
#   sprintf                       FAIL 327 (550/545) -> FAIL 322 (549/545)
# Every case's instruction count drops by exactly 1 (5-insn -> 4-insn
# collapse). The 6 cases where the raw diff COUNT went UP are all
# functions already >100%-mismatched (600-1400 diffs on 300-1400 insns,
# still WIP/floor-heavy); directly diffing ours()-before vs ours()-after in
# isolation (ignoring the oracle) proves the ONLY change in every one of
# those 6 functions is the intended, surgical jtbl collapse landing
# byte-identical to the oracle's `lui $at/addu $at/lw $at` shape -- the
# diff-count upticks are a difflib global-alignment artifact (removing one
# line anywhere shifts every downstream match in an already-saturated
# diff), not a real regression. The 5 cases close to PASS (FntPrint,
# PadInfoAct, sprintf, CD_get_intr, MemCardCmd_cb) show a clean, unambiguous
# diff-count DROP with no such noise.
#   - PadInfoAct: instruction count now matches exactly; the jtbl $at-fusion
#     shape is byte-identical to the oracle (sll-delay-slot / lui $at /
#     addu $at / lw $at / nop / jr). Remaining 20 diffs are a PRE-EXISTING,
#     unrelated register-coloring issue in this function (v1-vs-a2 /
#     a0-vs-v1 swaps after the switch), out of scope for this fix.
#   - Regression smoke, 0 regressions: 4 of the 22 explicit-form (non-$at)
#     jtbl fns (fixedatan FAIL 78/82/82, openfile FAIL 94/165/169, cluttype
#     PASS, shapetype PASS -- all byte-identical before/after) and 4
#     known-PASS non-jtbl fns in 2 clusters (PadSetActAlign, PadSetMainMode
#     -- libpad, same file as PadInfoAct; DeliverEvent -- libapi; atoi --
